==== Watch the Heart

#smallcaps[The] part of watchfulness against temptation that we have considered concerns the outward means, occasions, and advantages of temptation. Let us now go on to the part that concerns the #emph[heart itself], which temptation works on and entangles. Watching or keeping the heart, which we are bound to above all other keeping, also comes within the scope of this duty. For doing it rightly, take the following directions:

(1.) Let the man who would not enter into temptation labor to know his own heart, to be acquainted with his own spirit, his natural frame and temper, #emph[his lusts and corruptions], and his natural, sinful, or spiritual weaknesses. Then, finding where his weakness lies, he may be careful to keep at a distance from all occasions of sin.

Our Savior tells the disciples that “they did not know what spirit they were of”; under a pretense of zeal their spirit betrayed them into ambition and a desire for revenge. Had they known it, they would have watched over themselves. David tells us that he considered his ways: “I … kept myself from my iniquity” (Ps 18:23), the iniquity he was particularly prone to.

There are often advantages for temptations lying in men’s #emph[natural tempers and constitutions]. Some are naturally gentle, easygoing, easily persuaded, and pliable. This is the noblest temper of nature, and the best and choicest ground for grace to grow in when it is well broken up and plowed. Yet if it is not watched over, it will be a means of countless surprises and entanglements in temptation. Others are earthy, contrary, and morose, so that envy, malice, selfishness, peevishness, harsh thoughts of others, and grumbling lie at the very door of their natures; they can hardly step out without being in the snare of one or another of these. Others are hot-tempered, and the like. Now, the man who would watch so that he does not enter into temptation needs to be acquainted with his own natural temper, so that he may continually watch over the treacheries that lie in it. Beware that you do not have a Jehu in you, who will make you drive furiously; or a Jonah in you, who will make you ready to grumble; or a David, who will make you hasty in your decisions, as he often was in the warmth and goodness of his natural temper. The man who does not watch this thoroughly, who is not precisely skilled in the knowledge of himself, will never be free from one temptation or another all his days.

Again, just as men have particular natural tempers which, according to how they are attended to and managed, prove either a great #emph[fomes] \[tinder\] of sin or an advantage to the exercise of grace, so men may have #emph[particular lusts] or corruptions which, through their natural constitution or upbringing, and other prejudices, have taken deep root and gained strength in them. This, too, must be found out by the man who would not enter into temptation. Unless he knows it, unless his eyes are always on it, unless he observes its workings, promptings, and advantages, it will continually entangle and ensnare him. This, then, is our sixth direction of this kind: labor to know #emph[your own frame] and temper; what spirit you are of; what allies Satan has in your heart; where corruption is strong and where grace is weak; what stronghold lust has in your natural constitution; and the like. How many have had all their comforts blighted and their peace disturbed by their natural passion and peevishness! How many are #emph[made useless] in the world by their contrariness and discontent! How many are disquieted even by their own gentleness and pliability! Be acquainted, then, with your own heart. Though it is deep, search it; though it is dark, inquire into it; though it gives all its disorders other names than they deserve, do not believe it. Men are utter strangers to themselves. They give flattering titles to their natural disorders. They strive to justify, cover over, or excuse the evils of their hearts that suit their natural tempers and constitutions, rather than to destroy them, and by these means they keep themselves from taking a clear and distinct view of them. If it were not so, they could not possibly hang in the same briers all their days without attempting to be delivered. Uselessness and scandal in professors are branches that grow constantly on this root of not knowing their own frame and temper. And how few there are who will either study these things themselves or bear with those who would make them known to them!

(2.) When you know the #emph[state and condition] of your heart as to the particulars mentioned, watch against all occasions and opportunities, employments, company, times alone, and business that tend to entangle your natural temper or provoke your corruption.

Perhaps there are some ways, some company, some business, that you never in your life escaped without suffering by them, more or less, because they are so suited to entice or provoke your corruption. Perhaps you are in a state and condition of life that ensnares you day by day, on account of your ambition, passion, discontent, or the like. If you have any love for your soul, it is time for you to wake up and to free yourself like a bird from the evil snare. Peter will not be in a hurry to come to the high priest’s hall again; nor would David walk again on the roof of his house when he should have been in the high places of the field. But the particulars of this point are so various, and of such different kinds for different persons, that it is impossible to list them (Prov 4:14, 15). No small part of the wisdom that consists in ordering our conduct rightly lies in this. Since we have so little power over our hearts once they meet with provocations that suit them, we are to keep the two apart, as a man would keep fire apart from the parts of the house he lives in that will burn.

(3.) Be sure to lay up #emph[provision] in store against the approach of any temptation.

This also belongs to our watchfulness over our hearts. You#super[+] will say, “What provision is meant, and where is it to be laid up?” Our hearts, as our Savior says, are our treasury. There we lay up whatever we have, good or bad, and from there we draw it for our use (Matt 12:35). It is in the heart, then, that provision is to be laid up against temptation. When an enemy comes near a fort or castle to besiege and take it, he if he finds it well manned and furnished with provisions for a siege, and so able to hold out, often withdraws and does not assault it. If Satan, the prince of this world, comes and finds our hearts fortified against his batteries and provided to hold out, he not only departs, but, as James says, he flees: “he will flee from you#super[+]” (Jas 4:7). As for the provision to be laid up, it is what is provided for us in the gospel. Gospel provisions will do this work; that is, keep the heart full of a sense of the love of God in Christ. This is the greatest preservative against the power of temptation in the world. Joseph had this, and so at the first appearance of a temptation he cries out, “So how could I do such a great evil and sin against God?” And that is the end of the temptation as far as he is concerned; it takes no hold on him, but departs. He was furnished with such a ready sense of the love of God that temptation could not stand before it (Gen 39:9). “Christ’s love compels us,” says the apostle, to live for him (2 Cor 5:14), and so, as a consequence, to withstand temptation. A man may, indeed he ought to, lay in provisions of the law too: fear of death, hell, and punishment, with the terror of the Lord in them. But these are far more easily conquered than the other; in fact, they will never stand alone against a vigorous assault. They are conquered in convinced persons every day. Hearts stored with them will struggle for a while, but they quickly give up. But store the heart with a sense of the love of God in Christ, with the eternal design of his grace, with a taste of the blood of Christ and his love in shedding it. Get a relish of the privileges we have by it: our adoption, justification, and acceptance with God. Fill the heart with thoughts of the beauty of holiness, as Christ designed it to be the end, outcome, and effect of his death. Then, in an ordinary course of walking with God, you will have great peace and security from the disturbance of temptations. When men can live and plod on in their profession and are not able to say when they ever had any living sense of the love of God, or of the privileges we have in the blood of Christ, I do not know what they can have to keep them from falling into snares. The apostle tells us that the “peace of God” φρουρήσει τὰς καρδίας, “will guard your#super[+] hearts” (Phil 4:7). Φρουρά is a military word, a garrison; so φρουρήσει means “will keep as in a garrison.” Now, a garrison has two things that go with it: first, it is exposed to the assaults of its enemies; secondly, there is safety in it from their attacks. So it is with our souls. They are exposed to temptations and assaulted continually; but if there is a garrison in them, or if they are kept as in a garrison, temptation will not enter, and so we will not enter into temptation. Now, how is this done? He says, “The peace of God will do it.” What is this “peace of God”? A sense of his love and favor in Jesus Christ. Let this abide in you, and it will garrison you against all assaults whatever. Besides, there is something here in a special way that is also in all the rest of the directions: the thing itself stands in direct opposition to all the ways and means temptation can use to approach our souls. Striving to obtain and keep a sense of the love of God in Christ, by its very nature, heads off all the workings and insinuations of temptation. Let this be a third direction, then, in our watching against temptation: lay up a store of gospel provisions, which may make the soul a fortified place against all the assaults of temptation.

(4.) At the first approach of any temptation, since we are all tempted, the following directions are also suited to carry on the work of watching that we are pursuing:

\[1.\] Be always awake, so that you may make an #emph[early discovery] of your temptation, and know it for what it is. Most men do not see their enemy until they are wounded by him. Indeed, others may at times see them deeply engaged while they themselves have no sense of it at all. They sleep without any sense of danger until others come and wake them by telling them that their house is on fire. Temptation in the neutral sense, as it denotes a way, thing, or matter that is or may be used for the ends of temptation, is not easily discovered. Few take notice of it until it is too late and they find themselves entangled, if not wounded. Watch, then, to understand in good time the snares laid for you, to understand the advantages your enemies have against you before they gain strength and power, before they are incorporated with your lusts and have distilled poison into your soul.

\[2.\] Consider the #emph[aim] and tendency of the temptation, whatever it is, and of all who are concerned in it. Those who actively join in your temptation are Satan and your own lusts. As for your own lust, I have shown elsewhere what it aims at in all its workings and enticings. It never rises up without intending the worst of evils. Every working of it would be a full-formed enmity against God. So look on it, in its first attempts, as your mortal enemy, whatever pretexts may be made. “I hate” it, says the apostle (Rom 7:15), that is, the working of lust in me. “I hate it; it is the greatest enemy I have. Oh, that it were killed and destroyed! Oh, that I were delivered out of its power!” Know, then, that in the first attempt or assault of any temptation, the most cursed, sworn enemy is at hand and is setting on you, and that for your utter ruin; so that it would be the greatest madness in the world to throw yourself into his arms to be destroyed. But I have spoken of this in my discourse on Mortification.

Has Satan, who has a share in every temptation, any friendlier aim and intention toward you? To beguile you as a serpent and to devour you as a lion is the friendship he owes you. I will add only this: the sin against the law that he tempts you to is not the thing he aims at. His design lies against your share in the gospel. He would make sin only a bridge to get over to better ground, from which to assault you as to your share in Christ. The one who today perhaps will say, “You may venture on sin, because you have a share in Christ,” will tomorrow tell you, effectually, that you have none, because you have done so.

\[3.\] #emph[Meet] your temptation at its entrance with #emph[thoughts of faith] concerning Christ on the cross; this will make it sink before you. Allow no talks, no dispute with it, if you would not enter into it. Say, “ ‘Christ Jesus, who died’: he died for such sins as these.” This is called taking up “the shield of faith, with which you#super[+] can extinguish all the flaming arrows of the evil one” (Eph 6:16). Faith does it by laying hold of Christ crucified, of his love in that, and of what he suffered for sin there. Let your temptation be what it will, whether to sin, or to fear or doubt because of sin, or about your state and condition; it cannot stand before faith lifting up the standard of the cross. We know what means the Papists, who have lost the power of faith, use to keep up the form. They will sign themselves with the sign of the cross, or make crosses in the air, and by virtue of that work done they think they will scare away the devil. To put faith to work on Christ crucified is really to sign ourselves with the sign of the cross, and by it we will overcome the wicked one (1 Pet 5:9).

\[4.\] Suppose the soul has been #emph[surprised] by temptation and entangled unawares, so that it is now too late to resist its first entrances. What shall such a soul do so that it is not plunged into it and carried away by its power?

1st. Do as Paul did: beg God again and again to “take it away” from you (2 Cor 12:8). If you continue in this, you will certainly either be speedily delivered out of it, or receive sufficient grace not to be utterly defeated by it. Only, as I partly said before, do not occupy your thoughts so much with the things you are tempted to, which often raises further entanglements; but set yourself against the temptation itself. Pray against the temptation, that it may depart; and when that is taken away, the things themselves may be considered more calmly.

2dly. Flee to Christ in a special way, as one who was tempted, and beg him to give you help in this “time of need.” The apostle teaches us this in Heb 2:18: “Because He Himself suffered when He was tempted, He is able to help those who are being tempted.” This is its meaning: “When you#super[+] are tempted and ready to faint, when you#super[+] need help, and must have it or you#super[+] die, put faith to work in a special way on Christ as he was tempted. That is, consider that he was tempted himself, that he suffered by it, and that he conquered all temptations; and that not merely on his own account, since he submitted to be tempted for our sakes, but for us.” (He conquered in and by himself, but for us.) And draw help from him; indeed, expect it (Heb 4:15, 16). Lie down at his feet, make your complaint known to him, beg his help, and it will not be in vain.

3dly. Look to Him who has #emph[promised deliverance]. Consider that he is faithful and will not let you be tempted beyond what you can bear. Consider that he has promised a comforting outcome to these trials and temptations. Call to mind all the promises of help and deliverance he has made, and ponder them in your heart. And rest on this, that God has countless ways you do not know of to bring you deliverance. For instance:

(1st.) He can send an #emph[affliction] that will make your heart dead to the matter of the temptation, whatever it is. Then what was before a sweet morsel under the tongue will have no taste or relish for you; your desire for it will be killed. So it was with David. Or:

(2dly.) He can, by #emph[some providence], change the whole state of things from which your temptation arises, and so take the fuel from the fire and make it go out of itself. So it was with the same David in the day of battle. Or:

(3dly.) He can #emph[crush Satan under your feet], so that he will not dare to suggest anything more to your harm (the God of peace will do it), and you will hear from him no more. Or:

(4thly.) He can give you such a #emph[supply of grace] that you may be freed, if not from the temptation itself, then from its tendency and danger. So it was with Paul. Or:

(5thly.) He can give you such a comforting persuasion of #emph[good success] in the outcome that you will have refreshment in your trials and be kept from the trouble of the temptation. So it was with the same Paul. Or:

(6thly.) He can #emph[utterly remove] it and make you a complete conqueror. And he has countless other ways of keeping you from entering into temptation so as to be defeated by it.

4thly. Consider where the temptation you have been surprised by has made its entrance, and by what means, and repair that breach with all speed. Stop up the passage the waters have made to get in. Deal with your soul like a wise physician. Inquire when, how, and by what means you fell into this disorder. If you find that negligence, carelessness, or failure to keep watch over yourself lay at the bottom of it, fix your soul there. Bewail it before the Lord, repair that breach, and then go on to the work that lies before you.
